"Pairs subgroup means and ranges to monitor process location and within-subgroup variation under rational sampling.\n\nUse when: Small, consistently defined rational subgroups are collected repeatedly. Within-subgroup variation can support control limits for subgroup averages.\n\nAvoid when: Subgroup sizes or collection conditions vary without an appropriate limit calculation.\n\nVisual grammar: The first panel encodes each subgroup mean against ordered subgroup position. The second panel encodes each subgroup range with its own center and control limits.\n\nBuild: Define rational subgroups and preserve their acquisition order. Calculate subgroup means and ranges from a justified stable baseline. Use subgroup-size-specific constants to draw aligned mean and range panels with named signal rules.\n\nCheck: Check all observations belong to the correct subgroup. Verify control constants match the actual subgroup size. Inspect range-chart stability before interpreting mean-chart limits.\n\nPitfalls: Averaging can hide individual extremes and does not establish conformance to specifications. Do not use one fixed-size formula silently when subgroup sizes change.\n\nExample: Monitor synthetic batches sampled in groups of five, plotting each batch’s mean and range against baseline-derived control limits.\n\nAlternatives: Individuals control chart, EWMA chart, Box plot."
